# EPISODE 011: 2 Enoch (The Book of the Secrets of Enoch) — what Enoch saw in the ten heavens.
# Each entry: (kind, text, visual)
# Quotes (public domain only): King James Version (Genesis 5:24, 2 Corinthians 12:2-4);
# The Book of the Secrets of Enoch, tr. W. R. Morfill, ed. R. H. Charles (Oxford, 1896).
# Modern translations (Andersen in OTP 1983, Orlov, etc.) are copyrighted and are NOT quoted.

S = [
 # ---------------------------------------------------------------- COLD OPEN
 ("vo", "What did Enoch see in the ten heavens? An ancient book called Second Enoch says he was carried up through heaven, layer by layer, until he stood before the face of God. But here is the twist: the famous tenth heaven may not belong to the original book at all.", "pl_076"),
 ("vo", "This book survived only in Old Church Slavonic, copied in Russia and the Balkans many centuries after it was first written.", "ms_ch96_1"),
 ("vo", "And in 2009, a scholar found four pages of it in Coptic, from a fortress in Nubia. They are older than every Slavonic copy we have.", "da_585"),
 ("title", "THE SECRETS OF ENOCH|What Enoch Saw in the Ten Heavens", "da_675"),

 # ---------------------------------------------------------------- I
 ("chapter", "I|The Man Who Did Not Die", "dore_041"),
 ("vo", "Genesis gives Enoch only a few lines. He is the seventh man from Adam, the father of Methuselah, and he lives three hundred and sixty five years. Then his story ends in a single strange sentence.", "pl_113"),
 ("quote", "And Enoch walked with God: and he was not; for God took him.|Genesis 5:24 (King James Version)", "pl_113"),
 ("vo", "Every other man in that chapter dies. Enoch does not. The only other person in the Hebrew Bible who leaves this way is the prophet Elijah, carried off in a chariot of fire.", "dore_041"),
 ("vo", "So ancient readers asked the obvious question. Where did Enoch go? The Book of Enoch, the one quoted in the Letter of Jude, gave one answer. Second Enoch gives another, far more detailed.", "da_623"),

 # ---------------------------------------------------------------- II
 ("chapter", "II|A Book That Survived in Slavonic", "ms_ch96_t"),
 ("vo", "Second Enoch is also called the Book of the Secrets of Enoch, or Slavonic Enoch, because that is the only language in which it survives complete.", "ms_ch96_t"),
 ("vo", "More than twenty manuscripts and fragments are known, copied between the fourteenth and the eighteenth centuries. They come in a long version and a short one.", "ms_ch96_1"),
 ("vo", "The Russian scholar Popov first printed a full text in Moscow in 1880. A few years later, Professor Sokolov found another copy in the public library of Belgrade.", "ms_ch96_1"),
 ("vo", "In 1896 the book appeared in English for the first time, translated by William Morfill and edited by R. H. Charles at Oxford. Charles argued that behind the Slavonic lay a lost Greek original.", "ms_ch96_t"),

 # ---------------------------------------------------------------- III
 ("chapter", "III|Two Men at His Bed", "pl_164"),
 ("vo", "The story opens with Enoch at home, at the age of three hundred and sixty five. He falls asleep on his bed, and weeps in his dream, without knowing why.", "pl_164"),
 ("quote", "And there appeared to me two men very tall, such as I have never seen on earth. And their faces shone like the sun, and their eyes were like burning lamps.|2 Enoch 1:4-5 (tr. W. R. Morfill, 1896)", "pl_164"),
 ("vo", "They tell him that today he will ascend with them into heaven. Enoch wakes his sons, Methuselah among them, tells them not to look for him, and goes.", "pl_078"),

 # ---------------------------------------------------------------- IV
 ("chapter", "IV|Prisoners in the Dark", "pl_026"),
 ("vo", "In the first heaven he sees a great sea above the clouds, the angels who rule the stars, and storehouses of snow, ice and dew.", "da_585"),
 ("vo", "The second heaven is darkness. There he finds prisoners hanging, waiting for the final judgment. His guides explain that these are angels who turned away from God and followed their own prince.", "pl_026"),
 ("vo", "Then something unexpected happens. The fallen angels bow to Enoch, a human being, and beg him to pray for them.", "pl_198"),
 ("quote", "Who am I, a mortal man, that I should pray for angels? Who knows whither I go, or what awaits me?|2 Enoch 7:5 (tr. W. R. Morfill, 1896)", "pl_198"),

 # ---------------------------------------------------------------- V
 ("chapter", "V|Paradise in the Third Heaven", "pl_114"),
 ("vo", "In the third heaven Enoch is set down in a garden more beautiful than anything on earth. In the middle stands the tree of life, where God rests when He comes into Paradise.", "pl_114"),
 ("vo", "The guides say this place is prepared for the righteous, for those who feed the hungry, clothe the naked and help the orphan.", "dore_001"),
 ("vo", "But in the northern part of the same heaven lies a terrible place of darkness, fire and frost, prepared for the wicked. Heaven and its opposite, side by side.", "pl_141"),
 ("vo", "Charles noticed something about that garden. It matches a famous passage in which the apostle Paul describes a man taken up to the third heaven.", "da_623"),
 ("quote", "Such an one caught up to the third heaven... How that he was caught up into paradise, and heard unspeakable words.|2 Corinthians 12:2-4 (King James Version)", "da_623"),

 # ---------------------------------------------------------------- VI
 ("chapter", "VI|The Silent Watchers", "pl_015"),
 ("vo", "The fourth heaven holds the machinery of time: the paths of the sun and the moon, their gates, and the angels who pull them across the sky.", "da_619"),
 ("vo", "The fifth heaven is the strangest. Enoch sees countless figures like men, larger than giants, their faces withered, their lips silent. There is no worship there at all.", "pl_015"),
 ("vo", "These, his guides say, are the Grigori, the Watchers, who with their prince, Satanail, rejected God. Some of them went down to Mount Hermon, broke their vows, and took human wives.", "pl_173"),
 ("vo", "Enoch rebukes them. Why do you wait, brothers, and not serve before the face of the Lord? And they listen. Four trumpets sound, and the Watchers sing, with sadness and tenderness.", "pl_015"),
 ("vo", "In the sixth heaven, seven bands of shining angels keep the order of the world: the stars, the seasons, the rivers, and every living thing.", "da_631"),

 # ---------------------------------------------------------------- VII
 ("chapter", "VII|Seven Heavens or Ten?", "da_667"),
 ("vo", "In the seventh heaven Enoch sees the Lord from afar, on a lofty throne, with the hosts of heaven standing on ten steps around Him.", "da_667"),
 ("vo", "And now the twist. In the long version, Enoch keeps going: an eighth heaven called Muzaloth, a ninth called Kukhavim, and a tenth, Aravoth, where he sees the face of God.", "da_675"),
 ("quote", "In the tenth heaven Aravoth, I saw the vision of the face of the Lord, like iron burnt in the fire, and brought forth and emitting sparks.|2 Enoch 22:1, long version (tr. W. R. Morfill, 1896)", "da_687"),
 ("vo", "Charles marked the eighth, ninth and tenth heavens as a later addition. They are missing from the short version, and everywhere else the book speaks only of seven heavens.", "ms_ch96_x"),
 ("vo", "The idea of heaven in layers was common in the ancient world. Centuries later, Dante pictured nine moving heavens and a tenth beyond them. These engravings of his Paradise help us imagine what Enoch describes.", "da_557"),

 # ---------------------------------------------------------------- VIII
 ("chapter", "VIII|Like One of the Glorious Ones", "da_641"),
 ("vo", "Before the throne, God tells the archangel Michael to take off Enoch's earthly robe, anoint him with holy oil, and dress him in the raiment of His glory.", "da_641"),
 ("quote", "And I gazed upon myself, and I was like one of His glorious ones, and there was no difference.|2 Enoch 22:10 (tr. W. R. Morfill, 1896)", "da_641"),
 ("vo", "Then an archangel named Vretil, the wisest scribe of heaven, dictates to him. For thirty days and thirty nights Vretil never stops speaking, and Enoch never stops writing. The result is three hundred and sixty six books.", "da_693"),
 ("vo", "God Himself then tells Enoch how the world was made, secrets He says He never told even the angels.", "pl_207"),
 ("vo", "One of those secrets is a timetable. As creation took six days and a day of rest, the world will last six thousand years, then a thousand years of rest, and then an eighth day, a time with no years, months or hours.", "pl_209"),
 ("vo", "Charles believed this book is the first to spell out that world week of seven thousand years. Later Christian writers, like Irenaeus, used the same reasoning.", "da_619"),

 # ---------------------------------------------------------------- IX
 ("chapter", "IX|Thirty Days on Earth", "pl_210"),
 ("vo", "Enoch is sent back to earth for thirty days to teach his children before he is taken for good. His teachings are practical: feed the hungry, do not swear oaths, do not take revenge.", "pl_210"),
 ("vo", "One of them is unusual for its time. Animals have souls too, and at the final judgment they will testify about how people treated them.", "pl_209"),
 ("quote", "The Lord will not judge any soul of beast on account of man, but he will judge the soul of man on account of the souls of beasts in the world to come.|2 Enoch 58:4 (tr. W. R. Morfill, 1896)", "pl_209"),
 ("vo", "Charles wrote that he knew of no other pre-Christian Jewish writing with this idea.", "pl_164"),
 ("vo", "Some manuscripts add a final story. Before the Flood, a priest's wife named Sopanima dies, and her son is born after her death, already speaking, with the seal of the priesthood on his chest. His name is Melchizedek.", "pl_348"),
 ("vo", "God promises to send Michael to carry the child to the garden of Eden, so that he will not perish in the coming Flood.", "dore_004"),

 # ---------------------------------------------------------------- X
 ("chapter", "X|Who Wrote It, and When?", "ms_ch96_t"),
 ("vo", "Charles believed the author was a Jew in Alexandria writing between the years one and fifty, because the Temple in Jerusalem still seems to be standing in the book.", "ms_ch96_t"),
 ("vo", "Other scholars disagree. Some think the book we have is a much later Christian reworking. Proposed dates run from before Jesus to the tenth century.", "ms_ch96_22"),
 ("vo", "Then came the Coptic pages. Joost Hagen, a researcher at Leiden, recognized them among manuscripts excavated in 1972 at Qasr Ibrim, a fortress town on the Nile in Nubia.", "da_585"),
 ("vo", "They hold chapters thirty six to forty two, in the short version, and may date to the eighth to tenth centuries. So Second Enoch was being read in Africa before the Slavonic copies were made.", "da_557"),

 # ---------------------------------------------------------------- CLOSE
 ("chapter", "|Seven or Ten", "da_687"),
 ("vo", "So, what did Enoch see in the ten heavens? A prison of fallen angels, a Paradise with the tree of life, the silent Watchers, and at the top, a face like glowing iron.", "da_687"),
 ("vo", "But the oldest form of the book may have stopped at seven. Somewhere along the way, a copyist kept climbing.", "da_675"),
 ("vo", "The open question is who first wrote it, and where. If more Coptic pages turn up, we may finally know.", "da_585"),
 ("vo", "There are other lost books. The Book of the Wars of the Lord, which the Bible cites by name. The Protevangelium of James, the story of Mary's own childhood. And the Life of Adam and Eve, about what happened after Eden.", "pl_114"),
 ("vo", "If you want to hear their stories, subscribe, and tell us in the comments which one we should open next.", "da_693"),
 ("end", "THE LOST SCRIPTURES", "da_693"),
]
